        Use this tool when the user wants to create an ERD / database schema diagram
        from a set of entities (tables) and the foreign-key relationships between them.
        If the user provides raw SQL DDL, a CSV schema, or a Salesforce export, convert
        it into the structured `entities` / `relationships` form.

        The result is a real, editable ERD: each entity becomes an editable entity table
        and each relationship becomes a crow's-foot foreign-key line between the foreign
        key column and the column it references. The schema is stored as ERD data on the
        document, not as loose shapes.

        Before calling, check that every relationship the user described has a foreign
        key column on the referencing entity and a matching entry in `relationships`.
        When a table is split out to hold many rows per parent (e.g. a customer's
        address history), it needs a foreign key back to that parent, even if other
        tables also reference it.

        Example entities:
        [
            {"id": "user", "name": "User", "attributes": [
                {"name": "id", "type": "uuid NOT NULL", "key": "PK"},
                {"name": "email", "type": "varchar NOT NULL"}
            ]},
            {"id": "post", "name": "Post", "attributes": [
                {"name": "id", "type": "uuid NOT NULL", "key": "PK"},
                {"name": "author_id", "type": "uuid NOT NULL"}
            ]}
        ]
        Example relationships:
        [
            {"from": "post", "fromAttribute": "author_id", "to": "user", "toAttribute": "id",
             "fromCardinality": "zeroOrMore", "toCardinality": "exactlyOne", "label": "written by"}
        ]

        Returns:
            JSON with the created document details including document ID and edit URL


