Add a new file to this OS canvas. A "markdown" is a bundle holding one markdown file, `index.md`, for anything written; an "image" is a bundle holding one `metadata.json` that points at a hosted image, and it is created blank for the user to upload into; a "text" is a note drawn directly on the canvas as its own words, holding one `metadata.json` with a `text` field — create one only when asked for a label or a note, never to report on your own work. New work is a "prototype", scaffolded with no frames. Options on something are frames on a "canvas", never a new exploration. A "frame" is one screen: file it under the prototype it is a screen of with `parent`, or leave `parent` out for a standalone screen or sheet that belongs to no prototype. Wherever it is filed, any prototype plays it by importing it as `@os/<frameId>`. A "slide" is one screen of a slide deck: `kind: "slide"` with `parent` set to the deck. It is added to the end of `slides` in the deck's `deck.json`, which is what the deck plays: reorder `slides` to move it, and never make a `.slide` folder by hand. A variant of a slide is a slide too: copy it with `copyFrom` and a canvas as `parent`, and it stays a slide and is not added to `slides`. Taking one out means removing its id from `slides` and renaming `<id>.slide/` to `<id>.frame/`, with `canvas.type` set to `"frame"` and `canvas.treeParentId` deleted in its `metadata.json`. Any other file but a "text-annotation" may take a `parent` too, to file it in the tree. A file made with `parent` is placed beside it; under a canvas, `canvasPlacement` says where the canvas places it. A "canvas" is a view onto other files and holds nothing of its own: its `index.tsx` imports frames and prototypes as `@os/<id>` and lays them out in `export const placements = [...]`, with `place(Frame, { id, x, y }, props?)` for a file it imports, `place('<id>', { id, x, y })` for a markdown file or an image, and `note({ id, x, y, width }, text, { fontSize, fontWeight, color, align }?)` for words. Each `id` is unique within the canvas. An OS whose guide (`read_os_guide`) lays it out as pages is stricter: a canvas takes no `parent`; a frame always takes its prototype as `parent` and is never placed on a canvas, so something standalone is a prototype with one frame; and every prototype, slide deck, markdown file and image takes the canvas it lives on as `parent`. Returns the id to address it by — use that id as `osFile` in later calls. When the work is a new thing, call this as soon as you know it needs one — before you explore, not when you get round to writing. The file appears on the canvas the moment it is made, which is how the user sees the work begin; reading for minutes first leaves them watching nothing. Wait only while you still have to ask the user what to build, or cannot yet tell whether the work is new or a change to a file already there. A design made elsewhere, such as a Magic Patterns editor design at /c/<id>, cannot be linked or embedded on this canvas. To bring one in, read it with `read_magic_patterns_design` and write its code into a file created with this. Pass `copyFrom` with an existing OS file id to copy its kind and exact contents into the new file. Otherwise the new file is scaffolded with a placeholder, so write its real contents afterwards rather than leaving it as created. A file has no spot of its own: it is listed in the Files tree, and is drawn on a canvas only where a canvas file places it.

