// Pictures to generate from — the id of an image OS file on this canvas, or a Magic Patterns CDN URL. Use for a style to match, a subject to keep, or an image to edit. Omit to generate from the prompt alone.
referenceImages?: string[],
}
>,
// Also add each image to the canvas as its own image file. For images that are the deliverable. Leave it off for assets a design will reference by URL.
addToCanvas?: boolean,
// Prototypes layout only: the id of the file to file each image under in the tree, as its own image file — a prototype, or any other top-level OS file. Implies `addToCanvas`.
parent?: string,
// A short name for this conversation, which you choose once and pass unchanged on every call in it: a chat id, or any two random words. Without it every conversation you run on this project counts as one writer, so another window's files are treated as yours.
conversation?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Find files by path pattern inside an OS file. Matches against the full path relative to `path` (or to the OS file) and returns the matches sorted alphabetically. Supports `*` (any run of chars except `/`), `**` (any run including `/`), `?` (single char), and `{a,b}` alternation. Patterns are anchored to the full path and case-sensitive, e.g. "**/*.stories.*", "src/components/**", "**/*.{ts,tsx}". Hidden files are included, so `.storybook/` and `.cursor/` show up. Returns up to 200 paths per call; if there are more, the output gives the `offset` to continue from. Prefer this over paging through `list_files`, or `bash find`, when you know the shape of the path.

```ts
mcp__Magic_Patterns__glob(args: {
// The id of the project to run against.
osId: string,
// Glob matched against each file path, e.g. "**/*.stories.*".
pattern: string,
// The id of the OS file to look inside.
osFile: string,
// A directory inside `osFile` to search under, e.g. "sources". Paths come back relative to it.
path?: string,
// Skip this many matches, to page through a long result. Default: 0.
offset?: number,
// A short name for this conversation, which you choose once and pass unchanged on every call in it: a chat id, or any two random words. Without it every conversation you run on this project counts as one writer, so another window's files are treated as yours.
conversation?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Search file contents with a regular expression, across the whole OS by default or within one OS file when `osFile` is given. This is how you find where something is defined or used before editing it — searching the whole OS is cheap and cross-OS-file imports are common. Case-insensitive. Each match comes with its line number and 2 lines either side, which is usually enough to write an edit_file without reading the whole file. Returns up to 100 matches — if you hit the cap, narrow the pattern, or scope it with `path` or `glob`. Long lines are truncated to 500 characters. To pull a specific slice of a very long line, use `bash` with `cut -c` or `sed`; it has its own output cap.

```ts
mcp__Magic_Patterns__grep(args: {
// The id of the project to run against.
osId: string,
// A regular expression, in ripgrep syntax.
pattern: string,
// Restrict the search to one OS file. Omit to search the whole OS.
osFile?: string,
// Narrow the search inside `osFile`: a directory searches everything under it, a file searches just that file. Requires `osFile`.
path?: string,
// A file the user attached in chat, by the `upload` path its message listed, e.g. "abc123/pricing.html". Read-only, and not part of the OS. Cannot be combined with `osFile` or `path`.
upload?: string,
// Only search paths matching this glob, e.g. "*.tsx".
glob?: string,
// A short name for this conversation, which you choose once and pass unchanged on every call in it: a chat id, or any two random words. Without it every conversation you run on this project counts as one writer, so another window's files are treated as yours.
conversation?: string,
}): Promise<unknown>;
```

Search source files in a separate Magic Patterns design, or a file on another Magic OS, with a JavaScript regular expression. Use this to locate definitions and usages before reading whole files.

```ts
