See a design as it is on the canvas right now, your latest writes included: a screenshot taken live from the dev server the canvas is drawn from, with nothing built. Use it to check layout, styling or anything else that depends on how the design looks, and to show one part of it: `element` crops to one element, or outlines it in place, named by the line of source it starts on, and `interact` hovers over, focuses or clicks one first, to show a tooltip, hover state or what a click opens. `record` shows your user how it moves — an entrance animation, what `interact` does, or a short path through it with `steps` — as a short animated GIF, with a picture of its last frame for you to check where it ends up. Its result has the picture's URL. When it bears on what your user asked, show it to them in your reply: attach the picture itself wherever your chat can show one, and give its URL where it cannot. With `record` it is an animation: an image, not a video, so put it in your reply as a markdown image, which shows it playing. A page a little taller than the window is zoomed out to fit, so its bottom is not cut off. Takes a few seconds. When the live view is unavailable it falls back to a screenshot of the latest build, and says so.

