Answers a question about one known case from its docket: parties, lawyers, judge, case type, what was filed and when, how motions were decided, and where the case stands. It also finds which filings to read. Use searchCases first to find a case by party, lawyer or filing date, and analyzeCaseDocument for what a filing says.

Identify the case with ref, a caseId or Midpage docket URL from a tool, or with court and docketNumber when the user gives both; with only a docket number or a case name, find it with searchCases first. The entries that come back are the ones that bear on your question, so ask what you need to know.

refresh no, the default, uses the stored docket; a federal docket Midpage lacks is pulled from PACER, and a state one is asked of its court and returns pending, as it takes minutes to hours. refresh yes gets a fresh copy from PACER, counting toward PACER usage, or from a state court: use it when the user asks for the latest, or the stored docket is too old. If the plan or PACER usage blocks a refresh, the result is partial, from the stored docket, with the reason.

It returns case (caseId, caseName, court, docketNumber, dateFiled, status open or closed, dateTerminated once it ends, and url, the docket link); answer; up to 40 entries, each with entryNumber, attachmentNumber, dateFiled, description, documentId when Midpage has the filing, and stored false when reading it pulls it from PACER; asOf, the newest entry, and fetchedAt, which don't prove the docket is current; doesNotAddress; and downloadUrl for the docket report. Read an entry with analyzeCaseDocument, using its documentId, or the caseId as ref with its entryNumber and attachmentNumber.

Follow every nextStep, including on ok results and warnings, and retry only when retryable is true. If a PACER pull is refused, pass the message to the user; don't retry or look for another copy.

```ts
mcp__Midpage_Legal_Research__analyzeCaseDocket(args: {
// The court, as a Bluebook abbreviation or full name, like "S.D.N.Y.". Send with docketNumber.
court?: string,
// The docket number as the user wrote it, like "1:23-cv-04567". Send with court.
docketNumber?: string,
// What you need to know about the case, such as where it stands, how a motion was decided, or which filing to read.
question: string,
// A caseId from searchCases, searchOpinions or a monitor, or a Midpage docket URL, copied exactly. Send this, or court with docketNumber.
ref?: string,
// yes gets a fresh copy: from PACER for a federal docket, or from the court for a state docket it covers. Default: no, the stored docket; a federal docket Midpage doesn't have yet is still pulled from PACER. A PACER pull counts toward the user's PACER usage.
refresh?: "no" | "yes",
[key: string]: any,
}): Promise<{
accepted?: string | string[] | boolean,
// At most 6,000 characters.
answer?: string,
asOf?: string,
candidates?: { court?: string, id: string, name: string, year?: integer, [key: string]: any }[],
case?: {
caseId?: string,
caseName?: string,
court?: string,
dateFiled?: string,
dateTerminated?: string,
docketNumber?: string,
// open or closed, as the docket records it; absent when it doesn't say.
status?: "open" | "closed",
url?: string,
[key: string]: any,
},
doesNotAddress?: string[],
// A Midpage link to the docket report.
downloadUrl?: string,
// Up to 40 entries that bear on the question.
entries?: Array<
{
attachmentNumber?: string,
dateFiled?: string,
// At most 4,000 characters, ending in … when cut.
description?: string,
documentId?: string,
entryNumber?: string,
stored?: boolean,
[key: string]: any,
}
>,
fetchedAt?: string,
field?: string,
message?: string,
// Entries left out; ask a narrower question or name a date range for others.
more?: integer,
nextStep?: string,
reason?: "pacer_plan" | "pacer_usage" | "court_not_covered" | "too_many_lookups",
retryable?: boolean,
status: "ok" | "partial" | "invalid_input" | "not_found" | "ambiguous" | "unavailable" | "pending" | "error",
warnings?: { code: string, message: string, nextStep?: string, [key: string]: any }[],
[key: string]: any,
}>;
```

Reads one court opinion or filing and answers a question about it with verified quotations; for an opinion, each comes with a ready-to-paste citation and, when Midpage has one, a link to the passage. Use it before citing any case, and cite only what it returns. Use getCaseDocument instead for the full text or the original file, and searchOpinions first when you know a case only by topic or name.

Put everything you need in one question, since each call reads the whole document again; reuse what it returned, and call again only for a question it didn't answer. For a docket entry, send the caseId as ref with entryNumber, and attachmentNumber for an attachment; a federal entry not yet on Midpage is pulled from PACER if the account allows.

It returns document (ids, caseName, court, date, docketNumber, citation, url; for an opinion publishStatus, perCuriam, enBanc and precedentialNote; for a filing documentType, entryNumber, attachmentNumber and description), answer, and supportedPropositions: up to 20, each with the proposition and its conditions, a verbatim quote, citation (pinned when the page is known), deeplinkUrl, and opinionSection when it isn't the majority opinion. doesNotAddress lists what it can't answer; don't cite the document for those. treatment appears when the citator found Negative or Caution history, with citing decisions to read; its absence doesn't mean a case is good law.

Follow every nextStep, including on ok results and warnings, and retry only when retryable is true. If a PACER pull is refused, pass the message to the user; don't retry or look for another copy.

CITING: markdown links, exact returned text. A proposition: [its citation](deeplinkUrl ?? document.url); never add a pin cite. The case generally: [document.citation](document.url). Never cite a non-majority section as the court's holding. No id., supra, or WL/LEXIS cites of your own.

